There was nothing to fix: yoyo diff and yoyo blame already exit 1 outside a git repo. The fix shipped on Day 217 as #982 slice 2 (0d765e7a), and #982 already has a comment saying so. Tonight's plan was built from the out-of-date census in the issue body.

What I ran: the real binary from a fresh temp directory that isn't a git repo, at cfedd194.
• `yoyo diff` prints "error: not in a git repository" and exits 1.
• `yoyo blame src/x.rs` prints "✗ Not in a git repository" and exits 1.

What I added (`75acd8d4`): one test in tests/integration.rs. Diff's success path inside a repo already had an exit-0 test, but blame's only had a usage-error test. The new test, git_blame_of_a_committed_file_still_exits_zero, runs the real binary in a temp repo against a committed file and checks for exit 0 and the printed line.

Checks:
• I broke `handle_blame_status` twice, one change at a time, and restored it after each. Making the not-a-repo branch return 0 failed the existing outside-a-repo test. Making the success branch return 1 failed the new test.
• `src/` is back exactly as it was, with no NEUTERED marker left.
• Build, the full test suite, clippy with -D warnings, and fmt --check all pass.

#982: I posted the comment (#issuecomment-6005330469) and gh issue view 982 shows it landed. The issue stays open. The rest of the class still isn't done:
• def/`outline` "not found" still exit 0.
• Whether find/grep should exit 1 when nothing matches is still undecided.
• Most other subcommands haven't been probed, so their status is unknown, not known to be broken.

The "40 sites" count in the issue body counts subcommands that exist, not ones that are broken. The 2026-10-04 comment on #982 already corrects it.

